 psychological dispositions  who has relationships from birth through  life  who has duties who has particular kinds  of capacities or  incapacities all of those things  richly constitute who we take ourselves  to be  so when you ask you know who are you you  know  i myself think etc we are really talking  about this very rich and extended notion  of who we are  i think that there is no debate  uh philosophically that there is such a  thing about  human beings that as persons we have  this rich  psychology so then the the question  becomes and  was both common in ancient india and uh  has become so in modern western  philosophy in the last 40 50 years  what happens if some of the structural  features of your personal identity  are pulled apart what where you know  for example with amnesia or with  you know more uh exaggeratedly through  science fiction  suppose you had uh memories uh  transplanted to you  so then we start having boundary cases  and of course there is a rich  literature in psychiatry and about  disorders of  the self as it is called there which is  really about  the coherence and continuity of our  personhood  and i think that we have two options  there  either we go down the way of saying that  these kinds of undoubted uh  sort of psychotic possibilities that  human beings  encounter and the thought experiments  that allow us to think of  how our personhood might be twisted  and stretched and sometimes broken  either that suggests  that we have a very fragile view of who  we are  it's completely contingent on the ways  in which  our lives turn out things beyond us the  kind of traumas that might be  inflicted on us the pains and suffering  we might have to endure  as well as the kinds of things that  might happen to bits of our brain so  it's very fragile  or you can say yes of course that  there's that fragility  but there is so long as there is a  this underlying self this continuity  of perspective of consciousness then  there is something which 