's  chronologically specific or whether it's  culturally variable  we need to ask ourselves what is the  context  within which we are talking about these  variations  so some things it seems as if  evolutionarily there is  um certain kinds of continuities  which alone explain why  human beings have asked these questions  about who we are  as far as we can make out um throughout  history in different ways whether it is  in  you know classical sanskrit texts so  whether it is in  uh egyptian uh hieroglyphics whether  it's in different religious traditions  uh we people have asked these kinds of  questions which we can  see in the art in not only the  literature and the philosophical text  but in art and in ritual and in the  construction of  things like mausoleums and how we center  ourselves  in in urban planning so it's clear  that human beings have part of what  constitutes  the reflexivity of human beings is the  capacity  to ask who we are and that's relatively  unchanging if you then go back to what i  said earlier that  even you know as soon as we ask a  question about is there something like a  modern self  we're immediately saying you know we are  not talking about  the metaphysics of the self we are not  talking about necessarily the  phenomenological capacities  but we are talking about a particular  socio-political self as soon as we say  things like that we have in effect and  answers to why  our narratives of the self are so  mutable  so variable across time and across  cultures  which is that it is the context of our  enquiry into who we are  it's the purposes for which we enquire  who we are  it is the way we narrate uh and  communicate who we are to each other  that determines uh a particular local  answer so if there is anything  unchanging about the self  it is a human capacity to ask about the  self  but every answer you give is going to be  embedded and contextualized and  therefore local  and it seems as if evolutionarily we  have  developed we have evolved to have an  adaptive  concept of the self so for example if  you look at the buddha  he says there is no self in this in the  in in the context of  uh the the whether you know in the  context of saying  there isn't a diachronically um  continuous um unitary being  that generates all states of  consciousness  obviously if we are going to shift  the concept of no self to  modern um psychology you're going to say  people who lack a sense of self  are in conditions of psychiatric  breakdown  so the buddha wasn't saying let's embark  on a program  of psychiatric breakdown he was talking  about  the non-self in a particular context of  responding to  uh the conditions of suffering that he  saw so he said  on our suffering comes from a habit of  clinging to things and we cling to  things because we have  an over developed sense of who we are  such that we justify to ourselves  what we want and what we don't want and  when we don't get it as invariably in  life we don't  you have dissatisfaction and the  dissatisfaction generates more kinds of  actions which only deepen  this desire and that's what uh suffering  is in that context to say therefore  let go of who you think you are  therefore don't abide by sense of self  is very different from how a  psychiatrist might say  somebody has a breakdown in their sense  of self they don't have  continuous narratives anymore they don't  have agency they don't have capacities  to  function in life the buddha wasn't  saying that so  you can almost trivialize the deep  puzzlement about why we have  so many different you know changing  ideas of self by saying  it's all about the context and the  motivation under which we develop those  ideas  and that i think is perhaps one of the  sort of  really deep evolutionary advantages that  human beings have been able to  develop right since perhaps even pro  homo sapiens  because we're pre homo sapiens because  it is what has  allowed us to constantly locate  ourselves in relation 