th  cancer and i work with them to turn  their experience into  narratives and stories and that they can  share cancer places and not just cancer  you know terminal illness and  significant illness  places a real strain on people's sense  of self  i think it's a real challenge  to the identity that people have i don't  know you know i may be [ __ ]  long training here but i was i i i'd be  interested to hear you talk about  illness in the self so what is the  relationship  people have with themselves as they face  serious and especially terminal illness  there we are caught in i think a  profound dilemma  the more robust the sense of who you are  um your perhaps your religious identity  uh perhaps your conviction in an  afterlife  perhaps your awareness of  what kind of actions uh have led you to  this point  um such that you might be prepared for  death  whatever the that account the stronger  you have a sense of self  of yourself the  greater the  challenge you seem to face  about its extinction  on the other hand going towards death  um without  being too worried about what extinction  is  expecting that you  are simply going to seize  because you do not have a close grasp  on who you are might seem as if it is  therapeutically  preferable but it could have been  arrived at  only if you had had a life in which you  had an insecure grasp of yourself  and that and that leads to all kinds of  problems in relating to people in  directing your life and so forth  so there is this sense that the  condition of a good life  makes a good death more difficult what  might seem to make  a good death easier is incompatible with  most of what we think is a good life  so the transition is somehow  to arrive uh at  the uh threshold of death  with a robust sense of who we are  and what we have done and how  we have constituted ourselves up to this  point  but that at that point in  confronting the immediate inevitability  rather than the long-term inevitability  that we all have in confronting the in  the imminent inevitability of death to  somehow  transform that robust  grasp we have on who we are  into one that is more comfortable  with letting go and i think that  that uh  move perhaps psychologically comes  perhaps easier to some people than  others  which is why it seems as if you look at  people's lives  and you might not necessarily find  somebody  having lived a life of sufficient  reflexive discipline you just find some  people  more at ease with  the death coming and others who find it  much more terrifying i think part of  that  is simply to do with whether people are  so constituted  that they can make that transition from  having a robust sense of themselves to  nevertheless  feeling that that sense of self can be  let go  of if it is not possible there are some  people who might be inclined  to train themselves to  being at ease with letting go  but that could be actually terribly  dangerous  and painful for others for whom  that inclination isn't there that way of  confronting  the letting go of oneself at the  prospect of death  isn't what comes naturally and at that  point  we have absolutely nothing to do except  to be with them through that  inevitable point of extinction towards  which they fearfully travel  and then there is only sympathy  no therapy where do you  see our ideas of self or our idea of  self  going i'm not sure  whether the  novelty of history at this point  is sufficient to  [Music]  make me expect that there is anything  predictable  about changes in the ways people  think of themselves or narratives about  how people ought to think of themselves  changes two three reasons for that  one is that of course every generation  in every culture thinks of itself by  definition  as living in unprecedented time and  they're literally  right so that has always been the case  so the the novelty and the complexity  that  we look at will not be very different  in phenomenological terms how we feel  than how  the azt